# Upstox Historical OHLCV Provider

## What this is

An additive **historical** OHLCV provider for the existing Product Phase 6B
historical-data ingestion layer:

```
engine/data/historical_provider.py  ->  UpstoxHistoricalDataProvider
provider name                       ->  "upstox-historical"
API                                 ->  Upstox V3 Historical Candle API
```

It implements the **same provider contract** as `YahooHistoricalDataProvider`
(`provider_name` / `is_available` / `supports` / `fetch` →
`HistoricalProviderResponse`) and is available through the **same** service and
store pipeline (`HistoricalMarketDataService` → `HistoricalDataStore`). No
second storage format, no `data/upstox/` directory — a stored Upstox dataset
is indistinguishable from other valid historical OHLCV data except for the
`provider` field in its provenance record.

This is a **provider-integration task only**:

* No trading intelligence, decision, setup-detection, geometry, trade-planning,
  paper-trading, live-trading, Phase 6C/6D/6E/6F behavior is changed.
* The authoritative decision architecture is untouched.

## Environment variable

```bash
export UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN="<your Upstox Analytics API token>"
```

* The provider is available **only** when this variable is set.
* The token is sent **only** in the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header.
* The token is NEVER hard-coded, NEVER printed, NEVER logged, NEVER committed,
  NEVER written to tests/fixtures/docs/git-tracked files.

Missing token → `is_available() == False` and `fetch()` returns an honest
`ERROR` status response.

## Instrument-key resolution

Upstox requires **instrument keys** (e.g. `NSE_EQ|INE002A01018`), NOT Yahoo
symbols (`RELIANCE.NS` is not a valid Upstox key). Resolution is isolated to
the provider layer via an explicit mapping:

```python
"RELIANCE"  -> "NSE_EQ|INE002A01018"   # verified against the real API
"TCS"       -> "NSE_EQ|INE467B01029"   # NSE_EQ segment, Equities ISIN
"HDFCBANK"  -> "NSE_EQ|INE040A01034"
"ICICIBANK" -> "NSE_EQ|INE090A01021"
"NIFTY"     -> "NSE_INDEX|Nifty 50"    # NSE_INDEX segment, exact Upstox name
```

The mapping covers the **confirmed research universe** (NIFTY + the four
equities). Equities use the `NSE_EQ` segment keyed by the company ISIN; the
NIFTY 50 index uses the `NSE_INDEX` segment keyed by the exact Upstox index
name (`NSE_INDEX|Nifty 50` — the key contains a pipe separator and a space).

URL encoding: the instrument key is percent-encoded at URL construction time
with `urllib.parse.quote(instrument_key, safe="|")` — the pipe separator is
preserved (verified live for the equity keys) and the space in the NIFTY key
is encoded (`NSE_INDEX|Nifty%2050`), so the key stays a single URL path
segment. The end-to-end URL retains the Upstox V3
`/to_date/from_date/` ordering.

Only keys verified against the real API / the established controlled Upstox
verification are mapped. An unknown instrument raises `KeyError` (via
`resolve_instrument_key`) and `fetch` reports `UNSUPPORTED` — it fails clearly
rather than silently requesting an invalid Upstox key. The map is derived from
the existing canonical universe (`engine.config.universe`); **no second
universe** is introduced. To add an instrument key, extend
`_default_upstox_instrument_key_map()` or pass `instrument_key_map=` to the
constructor.

## Monthly chunking

A requested window (intraday OR daily) is split into **bounded monthly
calendar chunks** and every chunk is requested separately — a multi-year range
is never sent as one giant request. Example: `2022-01-01 -> 2023-01-01` becomes
12 monthly requests (`2022-01-01 -> 2022-02-01`, ..., `2022-12-01 ->
2023-01-01`). Chunk boundaries follow calendar months; no assumption that every
month has the same number of candles; missing trading days are never
fabricated; weekends / market holidays are not treated as errors.

Chunk responses are merged (dedupe by timestamp) and normalized to
chronological order BEFORE the canonical validation layer.

## Supported timeframes

| Timeframe | Upstox unit / interval |
|-----------|------------------------|
| `15m`     | `minutes` / `15`       |
| `1D`      | `days` / `1`           |

Only native Upstox intervals are used — no resampling / fabrication, and daily
data comes DIRECTLY from Upstox (never aggregated from 15m candles). The daily
endpoint uses the verified V3 spelling `days/1` (NOT `day/1`). Supported
aliases: `15m` / `15M` and `1D` / `1d` canonicalize via the existing timeframe
utilities. Other timeframes report `supports() -> False` and `fetch() ->
UNSUPPORTED`.

## Request details

```
GET https://api.upstox.com/v3/historical-candle/{instrument_key}/{unit}/{interval}/{to_date}/{from_date}
Accept: application/json
User-Agent: python-urllib/upstox-historical-provider
Authorization: Bearer <UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN>
```

* Finite default HTTP timeout (`30s`).
* No infinite retries — network/API failures are converted into the existing
  provider response vocabulary (`ERROR` / `EMPTY`).
* `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate` is set (the Upstox gateway rejects
  urllib's default `identity`).
* An explicit `User-Agent` is ALWAYS sent. The Cloudflare edge in front of
  `api.upstox.com` rejects urllib's default `Python-urllib/x.y` User-Agent
  with HTTP 403 / Error 1010 (`browser_signature_banned`), which previously
  made the live provider return an empty/error Upstox response even though
  the endpoint returned HTTP 200 when a real User-Agent (e.g. curl) was used.
  The `User-Agent` never contains the token.
* The response is validated before constructing `OHLCVCandle` objects:
  `status == "success"`, the `data.candles` list present (Upstox V3
  nests the candle list under `data`; a top-level `candles` list is also
  accepted for backward compatibility), row shape valid
  (`[timestamp, open, high, low, close, volume, open_interest]`),
  numeric OHLCV finite, timestamps parseable. `open_interest` is ignored —
  the canonical OHLCV contract has no field for it.

## Timestamp normalization

Upstox returns timezone-aware `+05:30` timestamps
(`2022-01-03T09:15:00+05:30`). They are normalized to UTC
(`2022-01-03T03:45:00+00:00`) before the canonical validation layer. Naive
timestamps are still rejected by the existing validator (the normalization
never weakens the canonical clock).

## Daily response normalization rule (`1D` / `days/1`)

A genuine Upstox **daily** candle is timestamped at `00:00:00+05:30` (IST) —
the exchange-day start label — per the official V3 daily-response examples
(`data.candles` rows such as `["2025-01-01T00:00:00+05:30", ...]`). Some daily
responses may nevertheless **embed intraday-related rows** (for example
`09:15:00+05:30` intraday timestamps that share the same trading day).

The deterministic, provider-specific rule implemented BEFORE canonical candle
construction is:

* For `unit == days` responses only, a row is a valid DAILY candle **iff** its
  IST-local time-of-day is exactly midnight (`00:00:00`).
* Every other row (any non-midnight IST time-of-day) is an embedded
  intraday-related row and is **FILTERED** — never stored, never repaired,
  never aggregated into a daily candle.
* Filtered rows are **counted** and reported in the fetch response reason, so
  no data is silently dropped.
* A daily response consisting entirely of embedded intraday rows yields an
  honest empty chunk (never fabricated daily candles).
* Malformed rows (bad shape / non-finite OHLCV / naive or unparseable
  timestamps) still raise `ValueError` and become an honest chunk `ERROR`.

Daily rows are also subjected to the same UTC normalization
(`00:00:00+05:30` -> `18:30:00` UTC of the PRIOR day) and duplicate dedupe as
every other timeframe. `15m` (minute) responses are NEVER filtered by this
rule.

## Date semantics

The requested `[start, end]` window from `HistoricalDataRequest` is respected
exactly: the number of monthly chunks is bounded by the months intersecting the
range, every chunk's `to_date` is capped at the requested `end`, and returned
candles outside `[start, end]` are dropped. No look-ahead behavior; no
silently extended range; `reference_now` future-rejection stays at the service
layer.

Intraday (`15m`) acceptance uses the strict UTC `[start, end]` comparison. For
DAILY (`1D`) responses the acceptance criterion is the candle's IST trading-day
label within the requested day labels `[start.date(), end.date()]` — this
matches the inclusive day labels the API was asked to produce. Without this
rule a genuine first-requested-day daily candle (UTC `18:30` of the PRIOR
day) would be wrongly dropped by a strict UTC comparison.

## Usage

```bash
# One real Upstox monthly ingestion (15m):
python scripts/ingest_historical_data.py \
    --instrument RELIANCE \
    --timeframe 15m \
    --start 2022-12-01 \
    --end 2023-01-01 \
    --provider upstox-historical

# One real Upstox daily ingestion (1D, days/1 endpoint):
python scripts/ingest_historical_data.py \
    --instrument TCS \
    --timeframe 1D \
    --start 2020-01-01 \
    --end 2023-01-01 \
    --provider upstox-historical
```

The CLI prints provider / requested range / records received / accepted /
rejected / validation status / storage info / reload verification using the
existing reporting structure.

## Authentication safety

* Token read lazily from `UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN` (constructor override exists
  only for tests).
* Token appears ONLY in the `Authorization` header.
* Tests assert the token never appears in the URL or in response reasons.

## Live verification procedure

```bash
export UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN="<token>"
python scripts/verify_upstox_live.py          # RELIANCE 15m 2022-12-01 -> 2023-01-01
python scripts/verify_upstox_live.py --data-dir /tmp/hist   # also makes a real ingestion
```

Demonstrates HTTP 200, non-zero candles, valid timestamps, valid OHLCV,
correct chronological normalization. Optional `--instrument` / `--timeframe` /
`--start` / `--end` flags are accepted. Exits 0 on success.

The normal test suite and the deterministic demo (`scripts/test_upstox_historical.py`)
never touch the network and require no token.

## Distinction between Yahoo and Upstox

| | Yahoo (`yahoo-historical`) | Upstox (`upstox-historical`) |
|---|---|---|
| Instrument identity | Yahoo symbols (`RELIANCE.NS`, `^NSEI`) | Upstox instrument keys (`NSE_EQ|INE002A01018`, `NSE_INDEX|Nifty 50`) |
| Availability | optional py packages (`yfinance`/`pandas`) | `UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN` env var |
| Timeframes | `1m`…`1D` | `15m`, `1D` |
| Range | retention-window clipped + windowed fetch | monthly calendar chunks |
| Timestamps | aware/naive normalized to UTC | aware `+05:30` normalized to UTC |

Yahoo remains the live/near-live provider for the dashboard; Upstox is an
additive **historical** provider. They share the same service/store contract.

## Limitations

* Implemented timeframes: `15m` (minute, `minutes/15`) and `1D` (daily,
  `days/1`). Other timeframes are honestly unavailable.
* Instrument-key coverage: NIFTY + RELIANCE / TCS / HDFCBANK / ICICIBANK (the
  confirmed research universe). Other instruments are honestly unavailable
  until their keys are verified and added to
  `_default_upstox_instrument_key_map()`.
* 15m historical availability on Upstox starts approximately 2022 onward;
  older 15m requests can legitimately return EMPTY and are never treated as an
  implementation failure. Daily data reaches back much further.
* Not a full historical corpus downloader — the provider does NOT download the
  whole universe, does NOT download years of data on startup, and there is NO
  scheduler for historical ingestion. Upstox does NOT automatically download
  data merely because the provider exists. Ingestion remains an explicit
  operator action through `ingest_historical_data.py` (or
  `verify_upstox_live.py` for a single one-month check).
* Not connected to Phase 6F.
* Provider-side rate limits / holidays are reported honestly; missing trading
  days are never fabricated.
* The daily-response normalization rule assumes the documented Upstox daily
  timestamp convention (`00:00:00+05:30`). If a future Upstox response format
  differs by historical era, the rule must be re-documented and re-tested —
  it is deliberately NOT extended to guess unknown formats.